WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:07.200
A new era for Europe’s most powerful economy.  
 
Sixteen years of a government helmed by Angela&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:13.440
Merkel is out - making way for a new, as of yet&nbsp;
unknown administration. While her tenure was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:19.760
defined by a steady hand amidst various crises,&nbsp;
many agree a bolder economic transformation is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:23.920
overdue. And that’s why the outcome of&nbsp;
the election matters for the world.  
 

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:30.000
When Germany says goodbye to the outgoing&nbsp;
government, the challenges to its economy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.280
will be carried over into the next one. There’s&nbsp;
a lot the incoming government has to deal with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:40.080
in order for Germany to hold on to its place&nbsp;
among the world’s leading economies.  
 &nbsp;

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:46.240
You know, I can I could list fifty five&nbsp;
different things that would have to be done 
 &nbsp;

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:51.760
There is still the recovery from the pandemic…. 
 
Population aging, digitalization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:55.360
the environmental crisis,  
 
 
...future relations with China... 
 
 &nbsp;

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:59.520
We’re not going to get to all of them.&nbsp;
But here are some of the biggest. 
 
 
 &nbsp;

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:05.760
In many ways some of these challenges are the kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:10.880
facing all of us. Like the one where we will&nbsp;
continue hurtling towards a catastrophic end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:14.240
if we don’t immediately cut back&nbsp;
on our greenhouse gas emissions. 
 

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:20.320
The effects of the climate crisis hit Germany&nbsp;
particularly hard just a few months ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:27.200
when unseasonably heavy rains and flooding killed&nbsp;
more than 180 people, destroyed entire towns and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:32.880
wrought tens of billions of euros in damage.&nbsp;
The catastrophe further fueled concerns that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:40.320
in the last years, too little had been&nbsp;
done for the climate...too slowly  
 

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:46.560
Germany certainly was not a climate&nbsp;
leader for the last decade under Merkel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:54.160
but in a sense, also we may be about to see&nbsp;
a restart, mean, the the news notably of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:58.080
decision by the German Constitutional Court&nbsp;
that demands action by the German government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:04.000
the introduction of a national carbon tax regime&nbsp;
that is comprehensive and covers all areas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:10.240
of German society last year, is very big news.&nbsp;
And it is the backdrop to the EU's decisions to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:15.040
attempt the same thing at the European level.&nbsp;
So Germany is acting as a conceptual leader.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:22.320
They're a pacesetter. But is it enough to address,&nbsp;
you know, the reality of the climate crisis?  
 &nbsp;

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:27.600
Good question. Especially given a trend that&nbsp;
should worry Germany’s next government. 
 
 &nbsp;

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:33.280
This year, Germany is expected to&nbsp;
record its biggest rise in greenhouse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:37.920
gas emission since 1990. 
 
 
 
 
Last year, the pandemic brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:43.440
public life to a standstill... the upshot&nbsp;
of which is that Germany met its emission&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:50.800
reduction targets in 2020 compared to the baseline&nbsp;
year of 1990. As the economy reopened THIS year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:57.040
the 2021 goal will be missed...making it harder&nbsp;
to achieve an even bigger cutback in 2030. 
 
 

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:02.960
The goal is for the country to&nbsp;
become climate neutral by 2045.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:07.680
While it’s generally trying to head into the&nbsp;
right direction, it needs to speed up...as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.800
well as integrate climate change adaptation&nbsp;
into the economy…and that requires investing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:18.640
in resilient infrastructure, housing, transport&nbsp;
and industry. Analysts warn that not doing so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:24.960
will cost Germany much more in the end.  
 
 
 
The question is to what degree we can manage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:33.120
changes and successfully transform the&nbsp;
economy or whether we'll be very unhappy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:39.440
with a situation where economic prosperity&nbsp;
is at stake or where climate change is having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:44.480
very negative consequences for the economy&nbsp;
because it's an unmanaged transformation.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:49.520
Now in transforming the economy&nbsp;
of a manufacturing superpower - an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:55.680
INDUSTRY makeover will be key. 
 
 
 
When it comes to the challenge of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:59.840
modernizing industry, there’s one German&nbsp;
sector where that’s particularly urgent.  
 
 
 &nbsp;

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:06.000
The automotive industry - the German economy’s&nbsp;
crown jewel - is largely considered to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:10.080
been slow to adapt to low-emissions&nbsp;
technology like electric vehicles….but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:16.000
in the last years, the outgoing government has&nbsp;
pushed billions in incentives and subsidies to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:22.240
help kick electromobility into high gear.  
 
 
 
Still, years of dithering mean others have gotten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:27.920
there first. In terms of market cap, American&nbsp;
electric carmaker Tesla has overtaken the big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:33.360
automotive players...including Germany’s&nbsp;
big three - Volkswagen, Daimler and BMW.  
 &nbsp;

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:40.720
While Germany’s big automakers might be in&nbsp;
the position to swing the transition into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:46.000
an electric future, there’s an entire&nbsp;
network of suppliers designed around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:49.600
gas and diesel engine powered-&nbsp;
models that have to transform too. 
 &nbsp;

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:54.800
But transformation comes at a price.&nbsp;
One-worst case scenario says that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.800 --> 00:05:00.560
the shift to electromobility can cost the&nbsp;
automotive sector half of its jobs by 2030.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:08.240
Now remember that goal to become climate neutral&nbsp;
by 2045? The transport sector will have to  
 
 
 &nbsp;

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:13.360
sink emissions especially dramatically&nbsp;
in order for that to happen - about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:18.400
forty percent within the next decade. 
 
 
So the next government will still have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:23.360
grapple with the question of whether to stop&nbsp;
the sale of conventional combustion engines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:28.640
in order to achieve that goal - something that&nbsp;
divides political parties and the population. 
 &nbsp;

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:34.640
Now we can’t talk about Germany’s most&nbsp;
exported product without zooming out onto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:40.400
trade itself. To say Germany has done well&nbsp;
from globalization is an understatement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:45.760
In terms of share of global exports, only the&nbsp;
world’s number one and two economies are ahead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:51.840
But the new German government will be taking over&nbsp;
at a particularly special time for global trade. 
 

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:57.360
Following the protectionism in vogue during&nbsp;
the Trump years, the COVID-19 pandemic also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:02.400
did its part in creating supply bottlenecks&nbsp;
and disrupting supply chains - resulting in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:08.320
shortages in building materials, vital tech parts&nbsp;
like semiconductors, plastics, rubber and metals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:16.080
Even as trade recovers, a discussion on how to&nbsp;
make supply chains more resilient is likely to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:25.120
continue onto the next administration. 
 
 
 
What they should not do is really to call for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:31.680
production reshoring and to call for firms&nbsp;
to to bring back production to Germany. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:40.400
what policymakers could do, for instance, is&nbsp;
to sign further trade agreements or to ratify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:46.960
already-negotiated trade agreements, such as&nbsp;
the trade agreement with Canada, the CETA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:53.600
or the trade agreement with South American&nbsp;
countries. With such trade agreements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.520 --> 00:07:06.400
these will lead to decreasing trade costs and&nbsp;
diversify their supply chain. This could be a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:12.240
to lead to more competitiveness of German firms.
 
But in the post-Merkel era,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:17.360
eyes will be on arguably the most significant&nbsp;
trading relationship this export-heavyweight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:23.200
has. CHINA is Germany's most important trading&nbsp;
partner, with goods traded between the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:32.160
countries reaching almost 213 billion euros in&nbsp;
2020. But Germany has been criticised for using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:38.240
trade as an engagement strategy in the face of&nbsp;
Chinese authoritarianism and expansionism. 
 

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:46.480
Germany can offer a vision, but it depends&nbsp;
critically on whether other key players are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:51.840
actually willing to go along with that, because it&nbsp;
is not the kind of power that can impose itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:56.880
I mean, how could Germany impose itself&nbsp;
on China? I mean, it's an absurd idea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:01.920
but it can offer a prospect. It can offer an&nbsp;
attractive partnership. And the Chinese have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:06.720
been only too ready to recognize the potential&nbsp;
benefits of that. You cannot in the end, if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:11.840
are an export-orientated manufacturing player like&nbsp;
Germany, ignore the sheer scale of that market.

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:17.200
At the same time, China is THE&nbsp;
key competitor to watch out for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:25.840
when it comes to digital technologies. For&nbsp;
its part, Europe has struggled to keep up. 
 &nbsp;

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:31.280
One other thing that the pandemic laid bare&nbsp;
was just how painfully behind Germany is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:36.800
when it comes to digitalization. Not what you&nbsp;
would expect from a country famed for invention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:41.760
and innovation. But internet speeds,&nbsp;
the digitization of public services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:45.440
and the development of digital business&nbsp;
models haven’t been fastest here. 
 

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:52.240
According to the Speedtest Global Ranking, Germany&nbsp;
isn’t even among the top twenty of countries with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:55.480
the fastest mobile and broadband speeds.
 

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:01.200
We will need to invest more in digital&nbsp;
infrastructure, but money is not going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:07.200
to solve all problems here. Again, it's like&nbsp;
putting money on the system as it is currently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:12.000
set up, is not going to necessarily help with&nbsp;
the complex decision making. It's not going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:20.080
help with the delay in finding consensus and then&nbsp;
maybe even going for a suboptimal, uncoordinated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:29.040
measure. Instead, it's really about changing the&nbsp;
system, how we manage this transformation. 
 
 &nbsp;

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:33.840
It’s not just because of slow internet&nbsp;
speeds that things take time in Germany.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:38.880
It’s the bureaucracy itself. There’s a&nbsp;
certain love for rules and processes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:44.000
often translates into piles of paperwork to be&nbsp;
filled out and transmitted via fax machines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:51.600
According to the World Bank, Germany ranks 125th&nbsp;
among 190 countries when it comes to the ease of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:55.840
starting a business. But experts warn that&nbsp;
more ambitious targets - say when it comes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.840 --> 00:09:59.600
to again, climate change regulations -&nbsp;
mean more rules are coming, not fewer.  
 
 &nbsp;

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:06.720
I expect that there will be more&nbsp;
regulations to be put into place. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:11.600
government needs to make sure that these&nbsp;
regulations don’t disadvantage Mittelstand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:15.760
businesses, small businesses, new&nbsp;
businesses, and at the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:21.120
they don't create new regulations and on the&nbsp;
other hand, create new support programs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:26.160
make sure that small businesses are more&nbsp;
disciplined. I think it's a huge task. 
 &nbsp;

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:32.960
And finally, the economy isn’t just about industry&nbsp;
and the policies that govern them. It’s about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:39.600
the people too. This country of 83 million is&nbsp;
experiencing a pretty key demographic change. 
 &nbsp;

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:46.080
Among all the age-groups here, it’s&nbsp;
the 40-59-year-old category that is by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:53.680
far the largest, at 23.6 million people. The&nbsp;
next-largest age group is made up of those 65&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.680 --> 00:11:00.880
years and older, at more than 18 million. 
 
 
This year alone, there will be roughly 150,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:06.160
fewer working age residents - that’s according&nbsp;
to the country’s federal labor agency.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:14.160
And to be able to address that shortfall, the&nbsp;
government has to do more to expand the pool. 
 &nbsp;

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:21.840
In terms of labor market, Germany must&nbsp;
strengthen the immigration of skilled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:29.440
workers and improve equal opportunities, for&nbsp;
instance, through education policy reforms.

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:35.440
And as people age out of the workforce,&nbsp;
they have to be supported by public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:41.840
finances - meaning higher spending&nbsp;
on health care and social security. 
 &nbsp;

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:47.840
Now these are just some of the challenges&nbsp;
facing Europe’s biggest economy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:52.240
But here’s the question. Is Germany’s next&nbsp;
government equipped to handle them? 
 
 
 &nbsp;

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:58.320
In the end, any kind of transformation&nbsp;
will have to be done within the context&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:03.040
of Germany’s consensus-seeking structure&nbsp;
- with the new government certain to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:08.240
broad coalition of parties representing&nbsp;
very different interests, the issue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:12.400
is whether it will be able to make&nbsp;
bold decisions on the big questions.
 &nbsp;

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:20.640
I think the biggest single weakness, which is&nbsp;
an inability or unwillingness to take risks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:28.320
in the economy and beyond.&nbsp;
The issue for Germany is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:40.800
combine the necessary and attractive checks&nbsp;
and balances in the German system that has led&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:49.360
this country to be seen as a bulwark for liberal&nbsp;
democracy, for stability in a world of horrific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:56.400
lurches and unpredictability.&nbsp;
How do you combine that with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.400 --> 00:13:05.760
a system that not just allows for but positively&nbsp;
encourages and facilitates greater risk taking? 
 

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:12.160
Creating conditions that encourage change for&nbsp;
the people is Germany's biggest challenge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:16.400
as it faces the big questions to&nbsp;
come, both locally and globally.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:21.520
In any case, change should be unavoidable.&nbsp;
But to what degree, and to what effect - will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.520 --> 00:13:27.040
depend very much on who assumes power, in what’s&nbsp;
still Europe’s economic powerhouse. For now. 
 &nbsp;

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:31.760
If you liked this video, we&nbsp;
have more just like it - so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:43.920
don’t forget to hit like and&nbsp;
subscribe. See you soon. 

